package play

// The token list: a `<ul>` of `<button>` elements, one per placement, and the
// accessibility source of truth for the tabletop (UI §7.6).
//
// # Why this is ordinary buttons in natural tab order
//
// §7.6 says "a `<ul>` of `<button>` elements, one per placement" and "ArrowUp /
// ArrowDown move focus between placements". Those are two different claims and
// this file answers them separately, because the second one is an *enhancement*
// over the first and the first is what the contract actually rests on:
//
//   - **Every placement is a real `<button type="button">` with no `tabindex`.**
//     That makes the list operable by the keyboard alone with *no JavaScript at
//     all*: `Tab` reaches every row in document order and `Enter`/`Space`
//     activates it. Phase 9's DoD item is "the token list is operable by keyboard
//     alone", and this is the property that satisfies it. It is asserted in Go
//     over the parsed DOM, so it cannot rot.
//
//   - **The arrow keys are an addition.** They move focus in the same order `Tab`
//     would, and they exist because a remote control and a screen-reader user
//     both want them (§7.5's row names the token list as one of the four places
//     arrows move between items). Removing the script costs a keyboard user
//     nothing that `Tab` did not already give them.
//
// What this file deliberately does **not** do is the roving-tabindex listbox
// pattern: it would take the rows out of the tab order, which means one button in
// the document is unreachable unless the script that made it unreachable is
// working. "Operable by keyboard alone" has to survive the script being blocked.
//
// # The four facts, and why they are inside the button
//
// §7.6 requires each row to name its placement, its hit points, its conditions and
// its layer. They are four `<span>`s inside one `<button>`, so the button's
// accessible name **is** the concatenation of the four — no `aria-label` to keep
// in step with the visible text, and nothing to get out of date. An `aria-label`
// on a row whose visible text changes is a second copy of the row's meaning, and
// the visible one is the copy nobody re-reads.
//
// The spans carry no `aria-hidden`, and there is no `aria-describedby`: the
// details are the name, not a description of a name that could have been shorter.
//
// # Selection is `aria-pressed`, and the server selects nothing
//
// Every row renders `aria-pressed` — `false` in every server-rendered document,
// because selection is client state (§7.6: "Enter selects and moves the camera",
// and "selection mirrors to the canvas"). The attribute is written out rather
// than omitted: a toggle button with no `aria-pressed` is not a toggle button at
// all, and writing the false value says "a selection exists and none is made"
// instead of saying nothing.
//
// `aria-pressed` rather than `role="radio"` in a `radiogroup` because a radio
// group is the semantics of *one choice from a set that should behave as one
// widget* — which is what a roving tabindex buys, and which would take the rows
// out of the tab order. Same decision as the listbox one above, taken once.
//
// # No `role="application"`, which is §7.6's prohibition
//
// The canvas is a PixiJS surface that will swallow keys if anything lets it, and
// `role="application"` is the switch that tells a screen reader to stop
// interpreting keystrokes. This package must never set it; phase 9's DoD says so
// again, and `TestNoRoleApplicationAnywhereInTheSurface` is the assertion.

import (
	"github.com/semiplane/semiplane/internal/web/components/ui"
)

// TokenList is the panel: a heading, a count, and the list.
//
// A `<section>` named by its own visible heading rather than a `<div>`, and the
// name resolves to a heading a reader can see — §7.2's rule is that a landmark's
// name resolves to something announceable, and a visible heading is the only
// version of that everyone else can see too.
//
// The panel is a *panel*, not a landmark: the rail around it is the landmark and
// this is one of its contents.
templ TokenList(view TokenListView) {
	<section
		class="panel token-list"
		aria-labelledby={ PanelHeadingID }
		data-state={ panelState(view.Placements) }
		data-testid={ PanelTestID }
	>
		<h2 id={ PanelHeadingID }>Tokens</h2>
		<p class="panel-note" data-testid={ CountTestID }>{ countLabel(view.Placements) }</p>
		if len(view.Placements) == 0 {
			// §4.7's rule that a section with nothing in it is absent, stated as a
			// sentence instead: the panel is present because the rail's tab is
			// present, and "Nothing on the table" beside an empty heading is the
			// state a reader needs rather than a widget that lied.
			<p class="empty" data-testid={ EmptyTestID }>
				No tokens have been placed yet.
			</p>
		} else {
			<ul class="card-list token-rows" data-token-list data-testid={ ListTestID }>
				for _, placement := range view.Placements {
					@TokenRow(placement)
				}
			</ul>
			<p class="panel-note" data-testid={ KeysTestID }>{ KeysHint }</p>
		}
	</section>
}

// TokenRow is one placement: a list item holding one button.
//
// The `<li>` carries nothing identifying. §10.2's hook rule is that a hook
// appearing more than once in a document selects nothing, and a row is the
// repeated element here — so the row is located by the `data-placement` on the
// button inside it, which is unique within a campaign because
// `realtime.PlacementID` is.
templ TokenRow(placement PlacementView) {
	<li class="token-row-item">
		<button
			type="button"
			class={ ui.TargetClass + " token-row" }
			data-token
			data-placement={ placement.ID }
			aria-pressed={ pressLabel(placement.Selected) }
		>
			<span class="token-row-name">{ nameLabel(placement) }</span>
			if placement.Kind != "" {
				<span class="token-row-kind">{ placement.Kind }</span>
			}
			<span class="token-row-hp">{ hpLabel(placement) }</span>
			if conditions := conditionsLabel(placement); conditions != "" {
				<span class="token-row-conditions">{ conditions }</span>
			}
			<span class="token-row-layer">{ layerLabel(placement) }</span>
		</button>
	</li>
}

// pressLabel is the row's `aria-pressed` value.
//
// Written as a function rather than interpolated from a `bool` so the two values
// are literals in Go and a reader can see that neither is empty. §10.2's
// reference rule holds it: `aria-pressed` is a token attribute, and the two legal
// values are `true` and `false`.
func pressLabel(selected bool) string {
	if selected {
		return "true"
	}

	return "false"
}